// The cleanup filter each body's mobile animation frames get when they are packed (udd-cli pack-ec-mobile-anims and
// pack-mobile-anims, the converter GUI). The renderer draws the packed frames as they are, so a change here needs a
// repack of the package it names. Study: docs/agent_notes/ec_frame_quantization.md.
//
// Syntax (KDL, read strictly: an unknown node, property or value is an error naming its line):
//
//   default package="ec" filter="highlight"   // each package's default, exactly once
//   body 12 package="ec" filter="median"      // one body of one package, at most once per package
//
// - package: "ec" (mobile_anim_ec.uddp, the Enhanced frames) or "cc" (mobile_anim_cc.uddp, the Classic frames).
// - body: the in-game (server) body id. The packer follows it to the stored frames the client draws for it (the
//   tileart.uop placeholder for Enhanced; mobtypes.txt, Body.def and Bodyconv.def for Classic) and filters those
//   frames, so every body sharing them shows them filtered. Two bodies sharing their frames must ask for the same
//   filter, else packing fails. A body the package has no frames for is a warning.
// - filter:
//   - "none": the frames as the client stores them.
//   - "highlight": a selective 3x3 median in clipped highlights only. Where at least 3 of the 9 window texels have
//     a channel at 250 or above (an overexposed render, where only the unclipped channels carry the shading and
//     its steps show as pale and saturated specks), a texel 12 or more (RGB distance) from its 8 neighbours'
//     channel median, and 2.5 times farther from it than the neighbours themselves, takes that median. Fine
//     texture elsewhere stays.
//   - "median": the same test on every texel. It removes more specks, and softens fine texture (scales, stone).
//   Both change only solid texels whose 8 neighbours are solid, keep alpha, the anti-aliased edge and the hue mask,
//   and leave a texel whose window mixes hueable texels with texels keeping their colour.
//
// The Enhanced default fixes the overexposed models (the red dragon among them) without touching textured bodies.
// Classic frames are 16-bit art without the clipped highlights; they stay as stored.

default package="ec" filter="highlight"
default package="cc" filter="none"
